  {
    "episode_id": 96370,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96370.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Listen, I'm a Philly guy, grew up playing hockey and was lucky enough to go into college and was a three-year captain. And then was blessed to go to the venture to law school and to teach at West Point. And to be young army captain, teaching the next generation of military leaders at, in my opinion, the world's premier leadership institution, the United States Military Academy at West Point. It was there. I was chairing the law of war team, competing internationally against China, Russia, and other nations, coaching those West Point cadets. But I was there when unfortunately 9-11 happened, when thousands of innocent Americans were murdered. And I quickly deployed in combat twice, came back from the second deployment, and then ran for the US Congress. It was the first Iraq vet in Congress in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, where I represented 700,000 folks. And then from that perch, you know, being on the Armed Services Committee, on the Intelligence Committee, and then becoming an appropriator was an awesome experience. And I did that for a few terms, and then came back home, had a TV show here with NBC in New York City at 30 Rock, and then was called back in the public service, political public service, to be the 32nd Undersecretary of the Army. It was the Under Secretary and then the Acting Secretary of the Army, and then to be in a Pentagon with great Americans who love our country and wake up every day to keep our family safe here at home was also a pretty",
