  {
    "episode_id": 96370,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96370.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, I come from a law enforcement family. So I was a federal prosecutor before joining the faculty at West Point. And then being there, and it really is just this incredible leadership lab. And I remember I would pinch myself, you know, being there and to be with these young Americans that they just inspire you every day. They're so bright. They're so committed. And, you know, at West Point, every cadet is an athlete and a scholar. And whether they play a Division I sport or they play club sport, everyone is expected to play something, but also everybody is expected to be a scholar. And so you look at some of the great leaders in our history, especially in current history. They're West Point graduates, or they touch West Point at some point. So for me, I'm not a graduate, but I now chair innovation at West Point, and I'm part of that team. But serving with people like David Petraeus, who was former professor there at the Social Science Department, or General Martin Dempsey, who was a Chairman of the D.C. staff, that when he was a one-star general, him and I were back in that Iraq together. He now is up in New York advising the NBA, if he's not down in his home in North Carolina. But just to see these leaders continue to serve at their time and service, that's what a soldier for life is, that leader of character committed to a donation for a lifetime of service. Sure.",
