  {
    "episode_id": 96370,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96370.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "I was, you know, I just signed a tiered contract to stay at West Point as a young Army captain. And I was already, had been through what's called Airborne School and Air Assault School, and I was getting ready for what's called Ranger School. And it was just a phenomenal experience for a blue collar guy like me to go on to go play hockey. The Commission as an Army Officer and then the teach at West Point was awesome, but what in our nation was attacked. I knew I wanted to get in the fight, so I deployed the combat. That was a phenomenal experience, but then it used that experience to go become the first Iraq veteran in Congress to co-sponsor the post-NIVN GI Bill to co-author it. And then in my second term, to the right to build that repealed, don't ask, don't tell, which, throughout 13,000 troops, because of who they love back at home, to me, it was pretty simple. You know, either believe in equality, or you don't? Sure. I do, and I was going to fight for it. But now, the next phase of... You know, some great American corporations were started by veterans coming back from war. So when you look at Walmart, instead of started by Sam Walton coming back from the Army in World War II, or Comcast, started by Ralph Roberts, the largest media company in the world, or Nike, the largest sports apparel company in the world, started by Phil Knight and Bill Bowerman, Army Officers. And the other generation of Vietnam, Fred Smith, Marine, and Vietnam coming back, starting FedEx. I look at that and I'd say, wow, have those GIs and what we're two came back and start doing small business. Now it's less than 5% of my generation of Iraq, Afghanistan veterans. So what can we do more for innovation? What can we do for entrepreneurship? I'm not just an investment community, but as a country, as a whole.",
