    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Sure, well, Iran struck back kinetically, and again, there's levels of warfare, so kinetic strikes, which are on CNN, you see it, there's explosions and there's blood, and I have lived through that, but... After that, response I ran did attack us in a base in Iraq. We had 15 American soldiers that suffered traumatic brain injury and were being treated overseas and then now back at home here Walter Reed. But also we've seen some retaliation and again that hybrid that below the kinetic threshold. So we've seen an uptake in cyber attacks from Iranian sources and again sometimes they're state actors and sometimes they're just citizens. that their government looks the other way. So in America, we are used to the rule of law, that known as above the law. So we wouldn't allow a young American sitting in his dorm room at NYU to attack Russia's IRA or GRU, their assets or intelligence assets. We would hold them accountable. But again, they're non-state actors, but we would not allow that to happen under our watch. What you are seeing is other countries, frankly, letting it happen. And that's international norm, that will not be tolerated. And we need to do a better job at harnessing the international community to ensure that we're not being injured as much as we are. And again, Mark, we're talking about 1% to 2% of our GDP. didn't some stats earlier. In 2017, it was about $2 billion in cyber premiums. Sure. There's about $114 billion in worldwide cyber security spending, and then about $5 billion in venture capital spending. So I have a small venture capital firm called Stoney Lonesome Group, where I invest in cyber companies, again, it's usually seed round, but again, making sure that we are innovating appropriately to defend our nation and to have these public-private partnerships.",
